BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:american-academy-berlin-prize-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T222052Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260926
SUMMARY:Deadline: American Academy in Berlin: Berlin Prize Fellowship 2027-
 28
DESCRIPTION:The American Academy in Berlin is an independent\, nonprofit in
 stitute for advanced study in the arts\, humanities\, social sciences\, la
 w\, journalism and public policy\, whose primary mission is to strengthen 
 the cultural and intellectual ties between the United States and Germany. 
 Each year\, after a rigorous selection process\, the Academy awards roughl
 y two dozen semester-long Berlin Prize fellowships to individuals who demo
 nstrate excellence in their fields. DISCIPLINES: applications are welcome 
 across the humanities\, social sciences\, law\, journalism and various are
 as of public policy. The Academy generally encourages projects addressing 
 pressing issues of the day\, specifically naming democracy\, security\, te
 chnology and society (especially AI)\, energy including climate\, and glob
 al order. Dedicated fellowships also support projects in Jewish studies\, 
 German studies\, and public health and biotechnology. Project proposals ne
 ed not focus on Germany\, but candidates should explain how a residency in
  Berlin would enhance their work. NOT ACCEPTED: proposals in mathematics o
 r the natural sciences. NOTE FOR ARTISTS: visual artists and composers are
  invited through separate\, nomination-based competitions\, so there is no
  open application route for them here. ELIGIBILITY: fellowships are restri
 cted to citizens and permanent residents of the United States. Both emergi
 ng and established figures are eligible. PhD holders qualify as emerging s
 cholars with a strong record of peer-reviewed work beyond the dissertation
 \; candidates in non-academic fields such as journalism\, filmmaking and p
 olicy need a significant record of publication or production\; fiction and
  nonfiction writers must have published at least one book with a reputable
  press. WHAT FELLOWS DO: fellows present their work to audiences in and ar
 ound Berlin through lectures\, readings\, public discussions\, performance
 s and film screenings\, and should be willing to engage with relevant Germ
 an institutions\, organizations and the media\; these activities are integ
 ral to the Academy's mission of fostering transatlantic dialogue. APPLICAT
 ION MATERIALS: a project proposal of 1\,500 to 1\,750 words plus a one-pag
 e bibliography\; a curriculum vitae of up to 10 pages\; two writing sample
 s totalling no more than 60 pages\; and three letters of recommendation\, 
 which are due 2 October 2026. DEADLINE: Friday 25 September 2026 at 12:00 
 noon Eastern Time (18:00 Central European Time). TIMELINE: all candidates 
 are notified of the competition outcome by email in late March 2027\; the 
 2027-28 Berlin Prize recipients are announced in May 2027.\n\nOrganisation
 : American Academy in Berlin\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arn
 hold Center. RESTRICTED: fellowships are open only to citizens and permane
 nt residents of the United States.\nAward: Monthly stipend of USD 5\,000\,
  plus roundtrip airfare\, accommodation at or near the Hans Arnhold Center
 \, partial board\, in-house library services and professional networking o
 pportunities. Roughly two dozen fellowships awarded each year. No applicat
 ion fee.\nDuration: One full academic semester (about 4 months). Fall runs
  mid-August to mid-December\; spring runs late January to late May.\n\nMor
 e: https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arnhold Center. RESTRICTED: fellows
 hips are open only to citizens and permanent residents of the United State
 s.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tba21-academy-tentacular-fellowship-ocean-space-venice-2026-2027@artifi
 cialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T222052Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260705
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260706
SUMMARY:Deadline: TBA21-Academy: Tentacular Fellowship 2026-2027 (Ocean Spa
 ce\, Venice)
DESCRIPTION:TBA21-Academy\, in collaboration with NICHE Centre for Environm
 ental Humanities and the UNESCO Chair on Water\, Heritage and Sustainable 
 Development at Ca' Foscari University\, is launching the first edition of 
 the Tentacular Fellowship\, an initiative-building fellowship based at Oce
 an Space and at Ca' Foscari in Venice. Conceived as a long-term platform f
 or artistic research and ecological engagement\, Ocean Space works to deve
 lop new forms of action capable of moving beyond awareness-raising to prod
 uce real\, tangible territorial effects through situated collaboration wit
 h the communities\, practitioners\, researchers and civic actors already w
 orking within the Lagoon of Venice. The fellowship invites a practitioner 
 or collective to initiate\, consolidate or expand a situated initiative in
  the Lagoon through the creation of a Tentacular Unit: a collaborative\, i
 nterdisciplinary platform able to gather artists\, students\, researchers\
 , institutions\, local communities and other actors around a specific soci
 o-environmental challenge. Through situated research\, alliance-building a
 nd public-facing formats\, the fellow helps build the social\, institution
 al and ecological conditions for the initiative to take root and grow over
  time. STRUCTURE: the two-year fellowship runs in two phases. In Phase 1 (
 incubation) the fellow is awarded a EUR 3\,000 grant to support independen
 t research and initiative development. In Phase 2 (activation) the fellow 
 is awarded a EUR 8\,000 grant plus a EUR 15\,000 activation budget\, for t
 otal fellowship support of EUR 26\,000. Details on the application process
 \, evaluation criteria and frequently asked questions are in the full open
 -call document. Application deadline: 5 July 2026\, 11:59pm CET. Apply via
  the online form linked from the open-call page.\n\nOrganisation: TBA21-Ac
 ademy\, with NICHE Centre for Environmental Humanities and the UNESCO Chai
 r on Water\, Heritage and Sustainable Development at Ca' Foscari Universit
 y\nLocation: Ocean Space and Ca' Foscari University\, Venice\, Italy\; sit
 uated work in and with the communities of the Lagoon of Venice.\nAward: To
 tal fellowship support EUR 26\,000. Phase 1 (incubation): EUR 3\,000 grant
  for independent research and initiative development. Phase 2 (activation)
 : EUR 8\,000 grant plus a EUR 15\,000 activation budget. No application fe
 e.\nDuration: 15 months\, September 2026 to November 2027\n\nMore: https:/
 /tba21.org/oceanspace-research-tentacular-fellowship?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://tba21.org/oceanspace-research-tentacular-fellowship?utm_source=
 artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Ocean Space and Ca' Foscari University\, Venice\, Italy\; situated
  work in and with the communities of the Lagoon of Venice.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:emergence-art-science-fellowships-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T222052Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260630
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260701
SUMMARY:Deadline: Emergence Art+Science Fellowships: Open Call for New Comm
 issions (Emergence Festival 2027)
DESCRIPTION:Emergence at All Scales (EAAS)\, a Dutch research consortium sp
 anning mathematics\, physics\, astronomy\, chemistry and related fields (f
 unded by NWO)\, invites artists\, makers and collectives to propose new wo
 rks inspired by the theme of Emergence: how complexity arises from simple 
 ingredients\, local interactions or microscopic rules. Selected projects a
 re developed in dialogue with EAAS researchers and premiere at the first E
 mergence Festival in Amsterdam in June 2027. EAAS studies emergence across
  scales\, from quantum spacetime and the early universe to hydrodynamics\,
  quantum matter\, collective behaviour\, social segregation\, networks and
  intelligent materials\; proposals may connect to one project\, several\, 
 or the theme broadly. WELCOMED FORMS: visual art\, digital art\, film\, vi
 deo\, documentary\, installation\, exhibition\, sculpture\; performance\, 
 theatre\, dance\, music\, sound art\; and hybrid or interdisciplinary work
 s that translate research questions\, concepts\, data\, simulations\, meth
 ods or early findings into a public experience\, ideally with a life beyon
 d the festival. FELLOWS RECEIVE: up to EUR 20\,000 production budget\, dia
 logue with EAAS researchers\, support from EAAS curator Marijn Bril\, poss
 ible university office/meeting space\, festival presentation\, and possibl
 e inclusion in the EAAS digital education and art database. REQUIREMENTS: 
 a clear connection to Emergence and EAAS research that speaks to non-speci
 alist audiences\; evidence of contact with EAAS researchers during proposa
 l development\; a presentable output at the June 2027 festival\; attendanc
 e at the EAAS annual meeting in March 2027 in Nijmegen\; and a realistic t
 echnical/safety plan for festival presentation. TO APPLY by 30 June 2026: 
 a proposal up to 3 pages (concept\, research connection\, proposed researc
 hers/hosts\, audience\, work plan)\, a one-page budget\, a CV\, and a port
 folio. Shortlist interviews July-August 2026. Questions: marijn@d-iep.org.
 \n\nOrganisation: Emergence at All Scales (EAAS)\, with The Science & Cock
 tails Foundation\, Stichting Paradiso and NWA Route Kunst\nLocation: Nethe
 rlands (Amsterdam\; premiere at the Emergence Festival)\nAward: Production
  budget up to EUR 20\,000 including VAT per project (covers artist fees\, 
 materials\, equipment\, space\, travel\, accommodation\, installation and 
 presentation). Total call budget EUR 80\,000 (around four projects). No ap
 plication fee.\nDuration: Projects start September 2026\; presentable outp
 ut ready late May 2027\; premiere June 2027\n\nMore: https://www.d-iep.org
 /artsciencefellowships?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=re
 ferral
URL:https://www.d-iep.org/artsciencefellowships?utm_source=artificialnouvea
 ugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Netherlands (Amsterdam\; premiere at the Emergence Festival)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:camargo-fellowship-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T222052Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261002
SUMMARY:Deadline: Camargo Foundation: Fellowship 2027-2028
DESCRIPTION:Founded by the artist and philanthropist Jerome Hill\, the Cama
 rgo Foundation has run this residency in Cassis on the Mediterranean coast
  since 1971\, and more than 1\,500 fellows have been through it. Fourteen 
 fellowships are awarded per cycle\, split roughly seven artists and seven 
 scholars or thinkers\, which is the point: the interdisciplinary cohort is
  the programme\, not a side effect. IMPORTANT: the Foundation has stated t
 hat this is the FINAL fellowship awarded through a public open call before
  it moves to a new selection process from 2027\, so if you have been meani
 ng to apply\, this is the cycle. RESEARCH\, NOT PRODUCTION: this is delibe
 rately structured as a research residency. You propose a project or line o
 f inquiry\, which can be development of a new work\, research preceding fu
 ture creative work\, or a broader investigation in your practice. Visual a
 rtists are explicitly reminded that research is prioritised over productio
 n\, and the Foundation supplies no art materials\, technical equipment or 
 artwork shipping\, so anything needing extensive fabrication is a poor fit
 . Fellows lead one resident session built around their own research or met
 hodology\, and attendance at these sessions is mandatory for everyone. Opt
 ional public Open Studios and introductions to organisations across the Ai
 x-Marseille-Provence region are also part of it. ELIGIBILITY: professional
  artists\, scholars and thinkers of any nationality. Artists should have a
 t least five years of professional practice and a mature body of work evid
 enced by exhibitions\, performances\, publications\, awards or grants. Per
 formance artists\, theatre directors\, choreographers\, multidisciplinary 
 artists\, writers\, filmmakers\, composers and visual artists are all elig
 ible within their categories. Collaborative applications of up to three me
 mbers are accepted. APPLICATION: four separate written responses covering 
 the project\, the research questions\, why Camargo specifically\, and why 
 now in your career\; reviewers are reading for a coherent research traject
 ory rather than a production proposal. An applicant webinar runs 1 Septemb
 er 2026. Deadline 1 October 2026 at 17:59 EST / 23:59 CET\; notifications 
 April 2027.\n\nOrganisation: Fondation Camargo\nLocation: Cassis\, France 
 (Mediterranean coast)\nAward: EUR 3\,500 stipend (EUR 350 per week for 10 
 weeks)\, basic transportation to and from Cassis\, a furnished apartment o
 n campus\, and a studio or appropriate workspace\, plus a reference librar
 y\, music and conference room and open-air theatre. Art materials\, techni
 cal equipment and shipping of artworks are NOT provided. No application fe
 e listed.\nDuration: Ten weeks. Fall 2027 session: 8 September to 17 Novem
 ber 2027. Spring 2028 session: 23 February to 4 May 2028.\n\nMore: https:/
 /camargofoundation.org/en/camargo-fellowship-2026-2027?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://camargofoundation.org/en/camargo-fellowship-2026-2027?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Cassis\, France (Mediterranean coast)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:canon-foundation-europe-research-fellowships-2026@artificialnouveau.git
 hub.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T222052Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Canon Foundation in Europe Research Fellowships 2026 (Eur
 ope–Japan Exchange)
DESCRIPTION:Canon Foundation in Europe Research Fellowships support bilater
 al research exchange between Europe and Japan\, awarding up to 15 fellowsh
 ips annually. AMOUNT: up to EUR 30\,000 per year (pro-rated for shorter st
 ays)\, flexible — covers living costs\, travel\, insurance\, research ex
 penses\, and books\; fellows determine their own allocation. ELIGIBILITY: 
 European nationals (including UK\, Israel\, Switzerland\, Turkey\, Balkan 
 and Baltic countries) conducting research in Japan\; or Japanese nationals
  conducting research in Europe. Applicants must have completed at least a 
 Master's or PhD degree within the last 10 years (older qualifications may 
 apply with supporting documentation). No restrictions on research field. P
 riority given to first-time applicants to Europe/Japan. Fellows arrange th
 eir own host institution and advisor. Up to 15 fellowships awarded annuall
 y. Deadline: 15 September 2026\, 23:59 CET (for fellowships starting Janua
 ry-December 2027). Apply at https://portal.canonfoundation.org/. Contact: 
 canon.foundation@canon-europe.com.\n\nOrganisation: Canon Foundation in Eu
 rope\nLocation: Europe or Japan (bilateral exchange\; fellows arrange own 
 host institution)\nAward: Up to EUR 30\,000 per year (pro-rated for shorte
 r stays). No application fee.\nDuration: Minimum 3 months\, maximum 1 year
 . Fellowships for 2027 start.\n\nMore: https://portal.canonfoundation.org/
 ?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://portal.canonfoundation.org/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Europe or Japan (bilateral exchange\; fellows arrange own host ins
 titution)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:imera-postdoctoral-residency-2026-27@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T222052Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260703
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260704
SUMMARY:Deadline: Iméra: 'This Is Not Another Fellowship' – Postdoctoral
  Residency 2026-27 (Aix-Marseille)
DESCRIPTION:Iméra's 'This Is Not Another Fellowship' is a 3-month postdoct
 oral residency at the Institute for Advanced Study at Aix Marseille Univer
 sité\, offered in two semester options: November 2\, 2026 – January 26\
 , 2027\, or March 1\, 2027 – May 28\, 2027. PROVIDED: free accommodation
  in one of fourteen campus apartments\; travel expenses covered\; desk in 
 shared open co-working space\; participation in the weekly Community Build
 ing Seminar (mandatory 3-hour Thursday sessions)\; access to interdiscipli
 nary scholarly networks. ELIGIBILITY: under 35 years old by May 28\, 2027\
 ; currently holding a postdoctoral fellowship contract at a university\; h
 ave not lived in France for more than 12 months in the prior 3 years\; if 
 requiring a visa\, must demonstrate sufficient funds. Research must align 
 with one of four Iméra programs: Arts & Sciences: Indisciplined Knowledge
 \; Interdisciplinary Dialogues\; Mediterranean\; or Necessary Utopias. All
  disciplines welcome. DEADLINE: 3 July 2026 at 1pm (CET+2). Apply at https
 ://www.imera.fr/en/this-is-not-another-fellowship-application-2026-27/.\n\
 nOrganisation: Iméra – Institute for Advanced Study at Aix Marseille Un
 iversité\nLocation: Aix-Marseille\, France (campus residency)\nAward: Fre
 e accommodation in campus apartment\; travel expenses covered\; office in 
 co-working space. No cash stipend specified. No application fee.\nDuration
 : 3 months: either November 2\, 2026 – January 26\, 2027\, or March 1\, 
 2027 – May 28\, 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.imera.fr/en/this-is-not-anoth
 er-fellowship-application-2026-27/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.imera.fr/en/this-is-not-another-fellowship-application-2026
 -27/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Aix-Marseille\, France (campus residency)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:columbia-ideas-imagination-paris-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T222052Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Columbia Institute for Ideas and Imagination: Paris Fello
 wship 2027-28
DESCRIPTION:Columbia University's Institute for Ideas and Imagination bring
 s a cohort of scholars and artists to Reid Hall in Paris for an academic y
 ear (September 2027 to May 2028)\, with a USD 75\,000 stipend\, housing\, 
 and research support. The creative-arts category explicitly includes visua
 l and sound arts and film alongside writing and composition. ELIGIBILITY: 
 any nationality\, but artists must not be permanently residentent in the U
 nited States (Netherlands-based artists qualify). Deadline 30 September 20
 26.\n\nOrganisation: Columbia University Institute for Ideas and Imaginati
 on\nLocation: Paris\, France (Reid Hall). Any nationality\; artists must N
 OT be permanently resident in the United States.\nAward: USD 75\,000 stipe
 nd\, plus housing at Reid Hall in Paris and office and research support\, 
 September 2027 to May 2028. No application fee.\nDuration: Fellowship year
  September 2027 to May 2028 at Reid Hall\, Paris.\n\nMore: https://ideasim
 agination.columbia.edu/apply/applying-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://ideasimagination.columbia.edu/apply/applying-for-a-fellowship/?
 utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Paris\, France (Reid Hall). Any nationality\; artists must NOT be 
 permanently resident in the United States.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ecf-academic-research-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T222052Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: European Cultural Foundation: Academic Research Fellowshi
 p (ECF Historical Archives)
DESCRIPTION:The academic track of the ECF Fellowship Programme\, which fost
 ers new research engaging with the history of the European Cultural Founda
 tion and the broader role of culture in European integration. The ECF was 
 established in 1954 by figures including Denis de Rougemont\, Robert Schum
 an\, Hendrik Brugmans and Prince Bernhard of the Netherlands\, on the beli
 ef that Europe will not be built by treaties alone. Over seventy years it 
 has produced programmes\, institutes\, campaigns and collaborations\, incl
 uding a contribution to shaping and implementing the ERASMUS student mobil
 ity scheme. That legacy is now preserved at the Historical Archives of the
  European Union in Florence alongside the records of the EU's main institu
 tions\, a placement that itself recognises the Foundation's role in Europe
 an integration. ELIGIBILITY for the Academic Research Fellowship: research
 ers in European studies\, cultural studies\, history and related fields ac
 ross the humanities and social sciences\, specifically PhD and postdoctora
 l researchers\, early-career researchers within ten years of completing a 
 master's or doctoral degree\, and independent scholars with an active rese
 arch profile. Fellows work at the HAEU embedded in the international schol
 arly community of the European University Institute\, with the archives ho
 used at Villa Salviati. Applications and all supporting documentation must
  be submitted in English through the online form. NOTE: this is the EUR 5\
 ,000 academic track. The EUR 10\,000 Artistic Research Fellowship\, open t
 o professional creatives in visual arts\, performing arts\, film and audio
 visual practices\, podcasting and audio storytelling\, documentary\, journ
 alism and literary practices\, is listed separately on the desk and shares
  the same deadline.\n\nOrganisation: European Cultural Foundation\, hosted
  at the Historical Archives of the European Union\nLocation: Historical Ar
 chives of the European Union\, Villa Salviati\, Florence\, Italy\, within 
 the European University Institute.\nAward: EUR 5\,000 grant to support a r
 esearch stay at the HAEU\, covering travel\, accommodation and living expe
 nses. The parallel Artistic Research Fellowship carries EUR 10\,000 and ad
 ditionally covers artistic production. No application fee.\nDuration: Rese
 arch stay at the HAEU. Outcomes must be completed and publicly presented o
 r published within two years of signing the Grant Agreement. Deadline 30 S
 eptember 2026 at 17:00 CET.\n\nMore: https://culturalfoundation.eu/open-ca
 lls/new-fellowship-programme-to-research-the-ecf-historical-archives/?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://culturalfoundation.eu/open-calls/new-fellowship-programme-to-re
 search-the-ecf-historical-archives/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&
 utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Historical Archives of the European Union\, Villa Salviati\, Flore
 nce\, Italy\, within the European University Institute.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
